WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:04.459
Sherlock Holmes and I were sitting near the fireplace in our Baker Street lodgings.

2
00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:07.637
"Would you like a pinch of snuff, Watson?" asked Holmes.

3
00:00:07.638 --> 00:00:12.296
The detective held out a gold snuffbox with a large amethyst in the center of the lid.

4
00:00:12.297 --> 00:00:17.461
The ornate box was such a contrast to Holmes' simple way of life that I had to comment on it.

5
00:00:17.462 --> 00:00:20.461
"Where did you get this snuffbox?" I asked.

6
00:00:20.462 --> 00:00:25.691
"It's a little present from the king of Bohemia in return for my assistance with the Irene Adler case."

7
00:00:25.692 --> 00:00:30.698
"And the ring?" I asked, glancing at the remarkable diamond that sparkled upon his finger.

8
00:00:30.699 --> 00:00:36.873
"It was from the reigning family of Holland for helping with a matter of extreme delicacy," said Holmes.

9
00:00:36.874 --> 00:00:40.604
"I can't confide the details to anyone, not even you, my dear Watson."

10
00:00:40.605 --> 00:00:43.945
"Are you working on anything special right now?" I asked him.

11
00:00:43.946 --> 00:00:49.598
"I have ten or twelve cases that are important," he replied. "But none of them are interesting."

12
00:00:49.599 --> 00:00:57.765
Holmes rose from his chair and went over to the window. "However I may soon have an interesting one. Look down there on the sidewalk."

13
00:00:57.766 --> 00:01:02.500
I went over to the window. A tall young woman stood across the street.

14
00:01:02.501 --> 00:01:06.262
She was wearing a black jacket trimmed with matching beads and fringe.

15
00:01:06.263 --> 00:01:12.662
Her gray, broad-brimmed straw hat was accented with a red feather, and a heavy fur boa was wrapped around her neck.

16
00:01:12.663 --> 00:01:17.908
"She seems to have some money, although not the best taste in clothing," I commented.

17
00:01:17.909 --> 00:01:22.076
The woman nervously peeped at our windows and played with the buttons on her gloves.

18
00:01:22.077 --> 00:01:30.650
Suddenly she plunged across the street and rang our doorbell. In a few moments our landlady, Mrs. Hudson, announced our visitor: Miss Mary Sutherland.

19
00:01:30.651 --> 00:01:35.483
Holmes welcomed the woman and asked her to sit down. Then he studied her for a minute.

20
00:01:35.484 --> 00:01:41.065
"With your poor eyesight, do you find that it's difficult to use a typewriter for so many hours a day?" he asked.

21
00:01:41.066 --> 00:01:46.129
"I did at first, but now I know where the letters are without looking," Miss Sutherland said quickly.

22
00:01:46.130 --> 00:01:52.791
Then, suddenly realizing what Holmes had said, she looked at him with both fear and astonishment in her eyes.

23
00:01:52.792 --> 00:01:55.791
"How did you know all that about me?" she asked.

24
00:01:55.792 --> 00:02:04.128
"I have trained myself to see what others overlook," said Holmes. "There is a small dent on either side of your nose that could only be made by eyeglasses.

25
00:02:04.129 --> 00:02:11.519
Your dress sleeves have purple velvet cuffs. I can see a line a little above your wrist, where a typist would press her arm against the table."

26
00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:13.939
"I am impressed," I said.

27
00:02:13.940 --> 00:02:20.924
"I also noticed that Miss Sutherland must have left home in a hurry this morning because she is wearing two boots that do not match," said Holmes.

28
00:02:20.925 --> 00:02:27.437
"Plus, one boot is fastened only at the first and second buttons, and the other at the first, third, and fifth buttons.

29
00:02:27.438 --> 00:02:34.614
But never mind all this; it's my business to notice these things. Why have you come to consult me, Miss Sutherland?"

30
00:02:34.615 --> 00:02:41.590
Looking a bit dazed by Holmes' powers of observation, Miss Sutherland blurted out her reasons for appearing at our lodgings.

31
00:02:41.591 --> 00:02:46.823
"A friend recommended you because you found her husband after the police had assumed he was dead," she replied.

32
00:02:46.824 --> 00:02:50.172
"Oh, Mr. Holmes, I wish you could do the same for me!

33
00:02:50.173 --> 00:02:55.087
I have an income of one hundred pounds per year in addition to the money I make with my typewriter.

34
00:02:55.088 --> 00:02:58.753
I would give it all to know what has become of Mr. Hosmer Angel."

35
00:02:58.754 --> 00:03:02.404
"Why were you in such a hurry to come here this morning?" asked Holmes.

36
00:03:02.405 --> 00:03:08.306
"Mr. Windibank—that is, my father—would not go to the police, and he would not consult you," said Miss Sutherland.

37
00:03:08.307 --> 00:03:16.052
"He wouldn't do anything and kept saying no harm had been done. That made me so angry that I just threw on my things and came straight here."

38
00:03:16.053 --> 00:03:22.147
"You and your father have different last names," said Holmes. "Mr. Windibank must be your stepfather."

39
00:03:22.148 --> 00:03:28.897
"Yes, he is my stepfather. I call him my father although that sounds strange because he is only five years older than I am."

40
00:03:28.898 --> 00:03:30.714
"And your mother is alive?"

41
00:03:30.715 --> 00:03:40.130
"Oh yes, Mother is alive and well. I wasn't pleased when she married again so soon after Father's death, especially when she married a man fifteen years younger than herself."

42
00:03:40.131 --> 00:03:44.614
Miss Sutherland explained that her father had been a plumber with a profitable business.

43
00:03:44.615 --> 00:03:48.225
With the help of the foreman, her mother continued running the business.

44
00:03:48.226 --> 00:03:52.369
"But when she married Mr. Windibank, he made her sell it," said Miss Sutherland.

45
00:03:52.370 --> 00:03:58.187
"He is in the wine business, working for the importers Westhouse and Marbank of Fenchurch Street.

46
00:03:58.188 --> 00:04:01.275
He felt that the plumbing business was beneath him socially."

47
00:04:01.276 --> 00:04:05.195
"Did your mother and stepfather get a good price for the business?" I asked.

48
00:04:05.196 --> 00:04:09.435
"They didn't get nearly as much as my father would have if he were alive," she replied.

49
00:04:09.436 --> 00:04:14.943
I expected Holmes to be impatient with these minor details, but he listened with the greatest concentration.

50
00:04:14.944 --> 00:04:20.683
"Your own little income of one hundred pounds a year," said Holmes, "did that come out of the plumbing business?"

51
00:04:20.684 --> 00:04:25.024
"Oh no, sir. It was left to me by my uncle who died in New Zealand."

52
00:04:25.025 --> 00:04:30.758
"I believe that a single woman can live quite nicely on sixty pounds a year," said Holmes.

53
00:04:30.759 --> 00:04:35.179
"Yet, between your inheritance and your typing income, you have more than that."

54
00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:39.752
Miss Sutherland explained that she could easily live on less than sixty pounds a year.

55
00:04:39.753 --> 00:04:44.490
But she handed over to her stepfather the full one hundred pounds, plus her typing income.

56
00:04:44.491 --> 00:04:51.078
"You must understand, Mr. Holmes, that as long as I live at home, I don't want to be a burden to my parents," she said.

57
00:04:51.079 --> 00:04:54.491
"Of course, they only have that money while I continue to live with them."

58
00:04:54.492 --> 00:05:00.806
"You have made your position clear," said Holmes. "Now tell us all about your connection to Mr. Hosmer Angel."

59
00:05:00.807 --> 00:05:05.806
A flush stole over Miss Sutherland's face, and she picked nervously at the fringe on her jacket.

60
00:05:05.807 --> 00:05:14.471
"I met him at the Plumbers' Ball," she said. "Mr. Windibank did not want Mother and me to go. He never likes me to go anywhere.

61
00:05:14.472 --> 00:05:19.875
"In fact, he gets quite angry if I even suggest anything as innocent as a Sunday school picnic.

62
00:05:19.876 --> 00:05:25.132
But I was determined to go. What right did he have to keep me from enjoying myself?"

63
00:05:25.133 --> 00:05:28.283
"I can't understand his objections at all," I said.

64
00:05:28.284 --> 00:05:32.624
"He said that the people at the ball would be beneath us socially," said Miss Sutherland.

65
00:05:32.625 --> 00:05:38.281
"And he tried to argue that I had nothing suitable to wear. But I had a new dress that I'd never even worn."

66
00:05:38.282 --> 00:05:43.622
Mr. Windibank finally stopped arguing with her only because his employer sent him to France on business.

67
00:05:43.623 --> 00:05:48.266
"So Mother and I went to the ball in the company of our former foreman," said Miss Sutherland.

68
00:05:48.267 --> 00:05:54.021
"And it was there that I met the gentleman called Mr. Hosmer Angel."

